WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.105
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:09.768
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.068 --> 00:00:17.190
Unit twenty-nine: Clauses of Manner and Comparison. Today you will compare real and unreal ways of doing things.

4
00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.968
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:24.131
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:25.845
As if.

7
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:30.161
As if means like, for unreal or uncertain comparison.

8
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.773
He talks as if he knew everything.

9
00:00:33.383 --> 00:00:35.618
Your turn. Say: As if.

10
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:39.398
As if.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:40.977
As though.

12
00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:44.285
As though means the same meaning as as if.

13
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:46.790
She looks as though she is tired.

14
00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:49.676
Your turn. Say: As though.

15
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:53.337
As though.

16
00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:55.131
Just as.

17
00:00:56.117 --> 00:00:58.395
Just as means in exactly the same way.

18
00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:01.935
Just as water flows, so time passes.

19
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.063
Your turn. Say: Just as.

20
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.017
Just as.

21
00:01:09.783 --> 00:01:10.657
The way.

22
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.359
The way means in the manner that.

23
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.299
I like the way you sing.

24
00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:18.687
Your turn. Say: The way.

25
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:22.523
The way.

26
00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:24.041
Like.

27
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.700
Like means similar to, followed by a noun.

28
00:01:28.283 --> 00:01:29.610
He runs like a deer.

29
00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:32.254
Your turn. Say: Like.

30
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:35.939
Like.

31
00:01:36.683 --> 00:01:37.510
As.

32
00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:41.097
As means in the manner that, before a clause.

33
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:42.905
Do as I say.

34
00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:45.797
Your turn. Say: As.

35
00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:49.437
As.

36
00:01:50.183 --> 00:01:51.707
Unreal comparison.

37
00:01:52.717 --> 00:01:56.260
Unreal comparison means comparing with an imagined situation.

38
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.606
An unreal comparison.

39
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:02.252
Your turn. Say: Unreal comparison.

40
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.740
Unreal comparison.

41
00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:08.984
Subjunctive past.

42
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:13.631
Subjunctive past means past form for unreal situations.

43
00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:15.903
He acts as if he were king.

44
00:02:16.517 --> 00:02:19.691
Your turn. Say: Subjunctive past.

45
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:24.127
Subjunctive past.

46
00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:28.514
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.313
Visal, complete the sentence: He behaves as if...

48
00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:46.532
He behaves as if he owned the place. As if is followed by a past tense for an unreal present, even though the meaning is about now. Were is used for all persons: as if he were king.

49
00:02:47.617 --> 00:02:49.489
What if the comparison is real?

50
00:02:50.583 --> 00:03:05.136
If the situation is likely, use the present: It looks as if it is going to rain. After as if, past perfect shows an unreal past: He spoke as if he had seen the accident.

51
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.843
How do like and as differ?

52
00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:25.049
Like is a preposition followed by a noun: He runs like a deer. As is a conjunction followed by a clause: Do as I say. In informal speech, like is often used before clauses, but formal English avoids it.

53
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.863
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:28.717 --> 00:03:35.383
Grammar focus: Manner and comparison: as if and as though plus past (unreal) or present (real).

55
00:03:35.683 --> 00:03:36.789
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:39.494
He behaves as if he owned the place.

57
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:42.290
It looks as though it is going to rain.

58
00:03:43.183 --> 00:03:45.624
She spoke as if she had seen the accident.

59
00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:55.883
As if / as though + past for an unreal present, past perfect for an unreal past, and present for a likely situation. Use like + noun, as + clause.

60
00:03:56.617 --> 00:04:00.546
Be careful! Don't say: He behaves as if he is king.

61
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.743
Say: He behaves as if he were king.

62
00:04:05.043 --> 00:04:06.625
Use were for unreal.

63
00:04:07.433 --> 00:04:09.807
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:11.833
Acting the Part.

65
00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:14.876
He walks into the room as if he owned the whole building.

66
00:04:15.867 --> 00:04:18.423
She speaks as though she had never made a mistake.

67
00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:21.979
It looks as if it is going to rain this afternoon.

68
00:04:22.967 --> 00:04:26.185
Just as a river shapes the land, habits shape our character.

69
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.987
Like a lion, he moved quietly and watched carefully.

70
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:36.137
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:36.983 --> 00:04:38.983
He talks as if he blank everything.

72
00:04:39.283 --> 00:04:42.623
A, knew. B, knows. C, will know.

73
00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:48.039
The answer is A: knew.

74
00:04:49.183 --> 00:04:52.648
She speaks as if she blank there. (unreal past)

75
00:04:52.948 --> 00:04:56.114
A, was. B, had been. C, is.

76
00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:01.608
The answer is B: had been.

77
00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.564
Which is formal and correct?

78
00:05:04.864 --> 00:05:10.340
A, Do like I say.. B, Do as I say.. C, Do like I'm saying..

79
00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.143
The answer is B: Do as I say..

80
00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:20.003
As if + past often expresses blank.

81
00:05:20.303 --> 00:05:24.653
A, an unreal situation. B, a habit. C, the future.

82
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.001
The answer is A: an unreal situation.

83
00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.602
According to the reading, what shapes the land?

84
00:05:34.902 --> 00:05:38.544
A, a river. B, the wind. C, the sun.

85
00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:43.821
The answer is A: a river.

86
00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:48.533
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:49.383 --> 00:05:51.766
Complete the sentence: 'He behaves as if…'.

88
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.074
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:02.374 --> 00:06:13.852
He behaves as if he were the manager. He gives orders to everyone and never listens. I use were because the comparison is unreal; he is not the manager.

90
00:06:14.152 --> 00:06:15.861
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:24.789
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and manner and comparison: as if and as though plus past (unreal) or present (real).

92
00:06:25.089 --> 00:06:30.122
Today's value is open-mindedness. Be open to new places, people and ideas.

93
00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:32.888
See you in Unit thirty. Goodbye!

94
00:06:33.188 --> 00:06:35.562
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

